          %div
            %h2#PolyakovGaugeStringDualitySection Polyakov gauge/string duality
            %p
              Key ideas underlying what is now known as
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+correspondence"} holographic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} duality in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
              and specifically as
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-QCD+correspondence"} holographic QCD
              (see also at
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+light+front+QCD"} holographic light front QCD
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polyakov"} Alexander Polyakov
              (cf. historical reminiscences in
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Polyakov08"} Polyakov (2008)
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge%2Fstring+duality"} gauge/string duality
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confined
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
              (the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap+problem"} mass gap problem
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/color+charge"} color
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+lines"}> Wilson lines
              ) between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
              as dynamical
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
            %p
              The logic here proceeds in the following steps (cf.
              %a{:href => "#Polyakov07"} Polyakov (2007), §1
              and see the commentary
              = succeed "):" do
                %a{:href => "#NoticeTheDecisiveInsight"} below
            .float_right_image{:style => "margin: 0px 0px 10px 15px"}
              %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
                %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HadronicStringInPionScattering.jpg", :width => "400px"}/
                %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                  From
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "string+theory#Veneziano12"} Veneziano 2012
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %strong flux tubes confine as dynamical strings
                %p
                  The starting point is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypothesis"} hypothesis
                  that the strong coupling of particles (such as
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
                  (such as the
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+nuclear+force"} strong nuclear force
                  = succeed "\u201D" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
                  = succeed "\u201D)" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+lines"} Wilson lines
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
                  with a given
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tension"} tension
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#KogutSusskind74"} Kogut &amp; Susskind (1974)
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a{:href => "#KogutSusskind75"} (1975)
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a{:href => "#Wilson74"} Wilson (1974)
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#Polyakov79"} Polyakov (1979)
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#Polyakov80"} (1980)
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a{:href => "#Polyakov80"} (1987)
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#MakeenkoMigdal81"} Makeenko &amp; Migdal (1981)
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "Nambu-Goto+action#Nambu70"} Nambu (1970)
                  %a{:href => "Nambu-Goto+action#Gotō71"} Gotō (1971)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                %p Under this assumption it would be:
                %ol
                  %li
                    %p
                      the
                      = succeed "/" do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tube"} flux tube
                      = succeed "\u2018s" do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tension"} tension
                      which keeps the particles at theirs endpoints
                      = succeed "," do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confined
                  %li
                    %p
                      the excitation of these
                      = succeed "/" do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
                      which follow
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Regge+trajectories"} Regge trajectories
                      (such as of
                      = succeed ");" do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadrons"} hadrons
                  %li
                    %p
                      the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering"} scattering
                      of these
                      = succeed "/" do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
                      which explains the observed
                      = succeed "," do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Veneziano+amplitudes"} Veneziano amplitudes
                %p which are the main qualitative features to be explained.
              %li
                %p
                  %strong quantum flux tubes probe effective higher dimensions
                %p
                  But if so, famous quantum effects make such
                  = succeed "/" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
                  behave like propagating in an effective/emergent higher-dimensional spacetime:
                %ul
                  %li
                    %p
                      either because one regards the string as
                      %em a priori
                      propagating in non-critical
                      = succeed "-" do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
                      = succeed "," do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mn 4
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4
                      = succeed "/" do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dilaton+field"} dilaton field
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Liouville+field"} Liouville field
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi ϕ
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                      appears to cancel the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomaly
                      and acts like a warped 5th dimension:
                    %p
                      this was the point of view published in
                      %a{:href => "#Polyakov81"} Polyakov (1981)
                      but later claimed, in
                      = succeed "," do
                        %a{:href => "#Polyakov08"} Polyakov (2008)
                  %li
                    %p
                      or because one regards the string as propagating in critical dimension (10 for the
                      = succeed ")" do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
                    %p
                      which is the point of view adopted later in
                      = succeed "," do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+duality"} AdS-CFT duality
                      %a{:href => "AdS-CFT+correspondence#Maldacena97a"} Maldacena (1997)
                %p
                  with only the endpoints of the
                  = succeed "/" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tube"} flux tube
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
                  constrained to lie in the original lower dimensional spacetime
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#Polyakov98"} Polyakov (1998)
                  %a{:href => "#Polyakov99"} (1999)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                %p
                  which now appears (in modern language that Polyakov did not originally use) as a “
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"}> brane
                  ” inside a higher dimensional
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bulk"} bulk
                  spacetime.
                %blockquote
                  %p
                    Notice that in this picture the observable physics that we set out to describe takes place
                    %em on the brane
                    (
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
                    which is typically flat
                    = succeed "!)" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+boundary"} asymptotic
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
                    of a higher-dimensional
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bulk"} bulk
                    spacetime, while the (potentially large) extra dimensions of a possibly
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∼
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
                    = succeed "-bulk" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS"} AdS
                    %em thickness
                    of the
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tube"} flux tube
                    = succeed "cf." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                    %a{:href => "#Polyakov08"} Polyakov (2008), p. 3
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              %li
                %p
                  %strong
                    large/small
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi N
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                    confined gauge theory is holographic string theory/M-theory
                %p
                  Thus the description of strongly coupled matter via
                  = succeed "/" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
                  now reveals a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holography"} holographic
                  situation where strongly-coupled quantum fields on
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+brane+models"} intersecting branes
                  are equivalently described by a theory of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
                  mediated by
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
                  propagating in a higher dimensional
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bulk"} bulk
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                %p
                  In relation to gauge string duality this is due to
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#GubserKlebanovPolyakov98"} Gubser, Klebanov &amp; Polyakov (1998)
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+duality"} AdS/CFT duality
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "#PolyakovRychkov00"} Polyakov &amp; Rychkov (2000)
            %blockquote
              %p
                While this dual bulk string theory is itself strongly-coupled unless the “number of coincident branes” is humongous (the “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large-N+limit"}> large-N limit
                ”) and thus unrealistic after all, the difference is that recognizing the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
                as physical objects reveals a web of concrete hints as to the string’s strongly-coupled (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"}> non-perturbative
                ) completion, going under the working title
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "AdS-CFT+correspondence#SmallNCorrections"}
                      AdS-CFT – Small
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi N
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                      corrections
              %p
                In summary, the plausible approach of understanding strongly-coupled quantum gauge theories by regarding their
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
                as dynamical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
                seems to recast the
                %a{:href => "mass+gap#ReferencesMassGapProblem"} Millennium Problem
                of understanding strongly-coupled matter into the problem of formulating
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+brane+models"} intersecting brane models
                of single (or a small number of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coincident+branes"} coincident
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-branes"} M-branes
                (such as the
                %em
                  %a{:href => "AdS-QCD+correspondence#WittenSakaiSugimotoModel"} Witten-Sakai-Sugimoto model
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
                system) on whose
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
                the desired strongly-coupled field theory is realized (such as
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            %p#NoticeTheDecisiveInsight
              Notice the decisive early insight of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polyakov"} Alexander Polyakov
              here: While the idea that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
              somehow describe
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadron"} hadronic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+states"} bound states
              was the very origin of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
              in the early 1970s (“dual resonance models”), the mainstream
              %em abandoned
              this perspective in the later 1970s when the critical dimension and the full spectrum of the string became fully understood (cf.
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goddard-Thorn+no-ghost+theorem"} Goddard-Thorn no-ghost theorem
              %em instead
              string should be understood as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GUT"} grand unified
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+everything"} theory of everything
              including
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
              (see e.g. the historical review of
              %a{:href => "string+theory#Veneziano12"} Veneziano (2012), esp. pp. 30-31
              which still clings to this perspective). From here it was only through the long detour of first discovering, inside this grander theory:
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
              (and
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-branes"} M5-branes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/near-horizon+geometry"} near-horizon
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+duality"} AdS-CFT duality
              just before the 2000s and then another decade of exploring
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+D-brane+models"} intersecting D-brane models
              that the community in the 2010s came back full circle to Polyakov’s holographic perspective on
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+QCD"} holographic QCD
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
              that propagate not (alone) in the observable 4 spacetime dimensions but in a primarily unobservable (meanwhile known as
              = succeed "-like)" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Randall-Sundrum+model"} Randall-Sundrum
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bulk"} bulk
              spacetime – a holographic description of reality that
              %a{:href => "#Polyakov99"} Polyakov (1999)
              referred to as the
              = succeed "," do
                %em wall of the cave
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Plato"} Plato
              (cf. also
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#Polyakov08"} Polyakov (2008), p. 6
            %p
              Our whereabouts in this remarkable picture are still often misunderstood today: If string theory is a theory of nature, then, it seems, we see the wall but not the cave: we live on a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowskian
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+D-brane+model"} brane intersection
              at the (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+boundary"}> asympotic
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
              of a primarily unobserved
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"} anti de Sitter
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bulk"} bulk
              – which may better be thought of not as physical space but as a configuration space of quantum flux.
          %h2#references References
          %div
            %h3#PolyakovGaugeStringDualityReferences Polyakov gauge/string duality
            %p
              Key ideas underlying what is now known as
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+correspondence"} holographic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} duality in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
              and specifically as
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-QCD+correspondence"} holographic QCD
              (see notably also at
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+light+front+QCD"} holographic light front QCD
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polyakov"} Alexander Polyakov
              (cf. historical remarks in
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Polyakov08"} Polyakov (2008)
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge%2Fstring+duality"} gauge/string duality
              (cf. historical review in
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "#Polyakov08"} Polyakov (2008)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confined
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
              (the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap+problem"} mass gap problem
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/color+charge"} color
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+lines"}> Wilson lines
              ) between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
              as dynamical
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
            %p
              Early suggestion that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confined
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
              is described by regarding the
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/color+charge"} color
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
              as
              = succeed "-like" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            %ul
              %li#KogutSusskind74
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Kogut"}> John Kogut
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leonard+Susskind"} Leonard Susskind
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Vacuum polarization and the absence of free quarks in four dimensions
                  %strong 9
                  (1974) 3501-3512
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.9.3501"} doi:10.1103/PhysRevD.9.3501
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              %li#Wilson74
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kenneth+G.+Wilson"}> Kenneth G. Wilson
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Confinement of quarks
                  %strong 10
                  (1974) 2445
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.10.2445"} doi:10.1103/PhysRevD.10.2445
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                %blockquote
                  %p
                    (argument in
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+gauge+theory"} lattice gauge theory
              %li#KogutSusskind75
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Kogut"}> John Kogut
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leonard+Susskind"} Leonard Susskind
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Hamiltonian formulation of Wilson’s lattice gauge theories
                  %strong 11
                  (1975) 395
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.11.395"} doi:10.1103/PhysRevD.11.395
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                %blockquote
                  %p
                    “The gauge-invariant configuration space consists of a collection of strings with quarks at their ends. The strings are lines of non-Abelian electric flux. In the strong coupling limit the dynamics is best described in terms of these strings. Quark confinement is a result of the inability to break a string without producing a pair.
                    = succeed "\u2026" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                    = succeed "\u201D" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                  %p
                    “The confining mechanism is the appearance of one dimensional electric flux tubes which must link separated quarks. The appropriate description of the strongly coupled limit consists of a theory of interacting, propagating strings.
                    = succeed "\u2026" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                    = succeed "\u201D" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                  %p “This picture of the strongly coupled Yang-Mills theory in terms of a collection of stringlike flux lines is the central result of our analysis. It should be compared with the phenomenological use of stringlike degrees of freedom which has been widely used in describing hadrons.”
              %li#Polyakov79
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polyakov"}> Alexander Polyakov
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em String representations and hidden symmetries for gauge fields
                  %strong 82
                  2 (1979) 247-250
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(79)90747-0"} doi:10.1016/0370-2693(79)90747-0
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              %li#Polyakov80
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polyakov"}> Alexander Polyakov
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Gauge fields as rings of glue
                  %strong 164
                  (1980) 171-188
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(80)90507-6"} doi:10.1016/0550-3213(80)90507-6
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                %blockquote
                  %p
                    “The basic idea is that gauge fields can be considered as chiral fields, defined on the space of all possible contours (the
                    = succeed ")." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+loop+space"} loop space
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+strings"} closed strings
                    should play the role of elementary excitations.”
              %li#MakeenkoMigdal81
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yuri+Makeenko"}> Yuri Makeenko
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+A.+Migdal"} Alexander A. Migdal
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Quantum chromodynamics as dynamics of loops
                  %strong 188
                  2 (1981) 269-316
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(81)90258-3"} doi:10.1016/0550-3213(81)90258-3
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                %blockquote
                  %p
                    “So the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/world+sheet"} world sheet
                    of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
                    should be interpreted as the color magnetic dipole sheet. The string itself should be interpreted as the electric
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tube"} flux tube
                    in the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monopole"} monopole
                    plasma.”
              %li#Polyakov87
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polyakov"}> Alexander Polyakov
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Gauge Fields and Strings
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1201/9780203755082"} doi:10.1201/9780203755082
                  %a{:href => "https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/50871"} oapen:20.500.12657/50871
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %blockquote
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                %a{:href => "https://phy.princeton.edu/people/alexander-polyakov"} old personal page
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                = succeed "1993?" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                were directed towards
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark"} quark
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
                = succeed "\u201C,which" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tube"} lines of force
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
                to the Coulomb law at small distances and would give permanent
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
                of quarks at large distances.”
            %p Cf. also
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexei+Morozov"}> Alexei Morozov
                , p. 6 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em String theory: what is it?
                %strong 35
                (1992) 671-714 [
                %a{:href => "https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1070/PU1992v035n08ABEH002255"}> doi:10.1070/PU1992v035n08ABEH002255
                ]
            %p
              Early suggestion, due to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Liouville+theory"} Liouville field
              seen in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosonic+string"} bosonic string
              via the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Polyakov+action"} Polyakov action
            %ul
              %li#Polyakov81
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polyakov"}> Alexander Polyakov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum geometry of bosonic strings
                %strong 103
                (1981) 207-210 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(81)90743-7"}> doi:10.1016/0370-2693(81)90743-7
                ,
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "http://qft.itp.ac.ru/polyakov-2.pdf"} pdf
            %p
              that such
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
              regarded as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confining
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
              are to be thought of a probing higher dimensional spacetime, exhibiting a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic principle
              in which actual spacetime appears as a
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+brane+model"} brane
            %ul
              %li#Polyakov98
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polyakov"}> Alexander Polyakov
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em String Theory and Quark Confinement
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/conferences/970718"} Strings’97
                  %strong 68
                  (1998) 1-8
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9711002"} arXiv:hep-th/9711002
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0920-5632(98)00135-2"} doi:10.1016/S0920-5632(98)00135-2
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                %blockquote
                  %p
                    “In other words the open string flies in the
                    = succeed "-space" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ,
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,\phi)
                    = succeed ".\u201D" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo =
                            %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi=0)
              %li#Polyakov99
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polyakov"}> Alexander Polyakov
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em The wall of the cave
                  %strong 14
                  (1999) 645-658
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9809057"} arXiv:hep-th/9809057
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0217751X99000324"} doi:10.1142/S0217751X99000324
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                %blockquote
                  %p
                    “We add new arguments that the Yang-Mills theories must be described by the non-critical strings in the five dimensional curved space. The physical meaning of the fifth dimension is that of the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
                    scale represented by the
                    = succeed ".\u201D" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Liouville+field"} Liouville field
            %p
              eventually culminating in the formulation of the dictionary for the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+correspondence"} AdS-CFT correspondence
            %ul
              %li#GubserKlebanovPolyakov98
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+Gubser"}> Steven Gubser
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Klebanov"} Igor Klebanov
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polyakov"} Alexander Polyakov
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Gauge theory correlators from non-critical string theory
                %strong 428
                105-114 (1998)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9802109"} hep-th/9802109
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0370-2693(98)00377-3"} doi:10.1016/S0370-2693(98)00377-3
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %blockquote
              %p “Relations between gauge fields and strings present an old, fascinating and unanswered question. The full answer to this question is of great importance for theoretical physics. It will provide us with a theory of quark confinement by explaining the dynamics of color-electric fluxes.”
            %ul
              %li#PolyakovRychkov00
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polyakov"}> Alexander Polyakov
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vyacheslav+Rychkov"} Vyacheslav Rychkov
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Gauge fields-strings duality and the loop equation
                %strong 581
                (2000) 116-134
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0002106"} arXiv:hep-th/0002106
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0550-3213(00)00183-8"} doi:10.1016/S0550-3213(00)00183-8
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %p
              and the suggestion of finding the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-QCD+correspondence"} string-QCD correspondence
            %ul
              %li#Polyakov02
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polyakov"}> Alexander Polyakov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Gauge Fields and Space-Time
                %strong 17
                S1 (2002) 119-136
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0110196"} arXiv:hep-th/0110196
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0217751X02013071"} doi:10.1142/S0217751X02013071
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %blockquote
              %p
                “in the strong coupling limit of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+gauge+theory"} lattice gauge theory
                the elementary excitations are represented by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+strings"} closed strings
                formed by the color-electric fluxes. In the presence of quarks these strings open up and end on the quarks, thus guaranteeing quark confinement. Moreover, in the
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi SU
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(N)
                gauge theory the strings interaction is weak at large
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux lines
                (strings) and not fields, thus returning us from Maxwell to Faraday. In other words it is natural to expect an exact duality between gauge fields and strings. The challenge is to build a precise theory on the string side of this duality.”
            %p Historical reminiscences:
            %ul
              %li#Polyakov05
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polyakov"}> Alexander Polyakov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Confinement and Liberation
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerardus+%E2%80%99t+Hooft"} Gerardus ’t Hooft
                (ed.)
                %em 50 Years of Yang-Mills Theory
                (2005) 311-329
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0407209"} arXiv:hep-th/0407209
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/9789812567147_0013"} doi:10.1142/9789812567147_0013
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/5601"} doi:10.1142/5601
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %blockquote
              %p
                “Already in 1974, in his
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "large+N+limit#tHooft74"}
                    famous large
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi N
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                    paper
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tube"} flux lines
            %ul
              %li#Polyakov07
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+M.+Polyakov"}> Alexander M. Polyakov
                  , §1 in:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Beyond Space-Time
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em The Quantum Structure of Space and Time
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0602011"} arXiv:hep-th/0602011
                  %a{:href => "http://www.solvayinstitutes.be/pdf/Proceedings_Physics/23rd_Solvay_Physics.pdf"} pdf
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              %li#Polyakov08
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+M.+Polyakov"}> Alexander M. Polyakov
                  ,
                  %em From Quarks to Strings
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0812.0183"} arXiv:0812.0183
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                %p
                  published as
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Quarks, strings and beyond
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paolo+Di+Vecchia"} Paolo Di Vecchia
                  et al. (ed.),
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em The Birth of String Theory
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511977725.048"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511977725.048
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                %blockquote
                  %p
                    “By the end of ’77 it was clear to me that I needed a new strategy
                    = succeed "for" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                    and I became convinced that the way to go was the
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge%2Fstring+duality"} gauge/string duality
                    = succeed "\u2026" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                    = succeed "\u201D" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                  %p
                    “Classically the string is infinitely thin and has only transverse oscillations. But when I quantized it there was a surprise – an extra, longitudinal mode, which appears due to the quantum ”thickening“ of the string. This new field is called the
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Liouville+field"} Liouville mode
                    = succeed "\u2026" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                    = succeed "\u201D" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                  %p
                    “I kept thinking about
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge%2Fstring+duality"} gauge/strings dualities
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Liouville+field"} Liouville mode
                    was discovered it became clear to many people including myself that its natural interpretation is that random surfaces in 4d are described by the strings flying in 5d with the Liouville field playing the role of the fifth dimension. The precise meaning of this statement is that the wave function of the general string state depends on the four center of mass coordinates and also on the fifth, the Liouville one. In the case of minimal models this extra dimension is related to the matrix eigenvalues and the resulting space is flat.”
                  %p
                    “Since this 5d space must contain the flat 4d subspace in which the gauge theory resides, the natural ansatz for the metric is just the Friedman universe with a certain warp factor. This factor must be determined from the conditions of conformal symmetry on the world sheet. Its dependence on the Liouville mode must be related to the renormalization group flow. As a result we arrive at a fascinating picture – our 4d world is a projection of a more fundamental 5d string theory.
                    = succeed "\u2026" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                    = succeed "\u201D" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                  %p
                    “At this point I was certain that I have found the right language for the
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge%2Fstring+duality"} gauge/string duality
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a{:href => "#Polyakov98"} Polyakov 1998
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9702076"} Klebanov 1997
                    in which he related
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D3+branes"} D3 branes
                    described by the supersymmetric Yang Mills theory to the same object described by supergravity. Unfortunately I wrongly thought that the paper is related to matrix theory and I was skeptical about this subject. As a result I have missed this paper which would provide me with a nice special case of my program. This special case was presented little later in full generality by Juan Maldacena (
                    %a{:href => "AdS-CFT+correspondence#Maldacena97a"}> Maldacena 1997
                    ) and his work opened the flood gates.”
            %p A detailed monograph:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/N.+D.+Hari+Dass"}> N. D. Hari Dass
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Strings to Strings – Yang-Mills Flux Tubes, QCD Strings and Effective String Theories
                  = succeed "," do
                    %strong 1018
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-35358-1"} doi:10.1007/978-3-031-35358-1
                %p Ch. 23:
                %p
                  %em Effective String Theories (EST) of Yang-Mills Flux Tubes
                  [
                  %a{:hraf => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-35358-1_22"}> doi:10.1007/978-3-031-35358-1_22
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.10629"} arXiv:2312.10629
        .revisedby
